---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout
  title="Morning Briefing - 08/11/26: Power Grab TX"
  description="Fort Worth's council votes tonight on a 90-day data-center moratorium as Amazon confirms an 8,000-acre, 7.65GW gas-fired AI campus in Pecos County."
>
  <div class="post-page">
    <div class="post-content">
      <h1>⭐ Morning Briefing — Texas &amp; DFW AI Data Centers, Grid, Land Use</h1>
      <p class="briefing-subtitle">(Latest updates through August 11, 2026)</p>

      <hr class="section-divider" />

      <h2>🔌 1. ERCOT &amp; Grid Signals</h2>
      <p class="section-tagline">A Compliance Wave Breaks Out While Trump and Abbott Spar Over the Pause</p>
      <ul>
        <li>President Trump publicly called Gov. Greg Abbott's Aug. 3 data-center interconnection pause "a mistake," saying communities that want data centers should be able to have them; Abbott has defended the audit as necessary to protect grid reliability, water supplies, and ratepayers (CBS Austin/KEYE, Aug. 9; Houston Public Media, Aug. 10; FOX, Aug. 8).</li>
        <li>In the days since, a string of major operators has publicly agreed to comply with Abbott's Aug. 3 standards — full-cost payment for grid infrastructure, water-efficient cooling, and usage disclosure — including Meta (Aug. 10-11), OpenAI (Office of the Texas Governor release, Aug. 10), Equinix (Aug. 11, citing more than $1.6 billion in Texas investment), Compass Datacenters (Aug. 10), QTS Data Centers (Aug. 6), and Montera Infrastructure (Aug. 7).</li>
        <li>ERCOT's request for a good-cause exception on Batch Zero classification timelines remains queued for the Public Utility Commission of Texas's Aug. 20 open meeting; no new large-load classification notices have posted since the Aug. 3 suspension (ERCOT Market Notice M-A080326-01).</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="signal"><strong>Today's Signal:</strong> The Abbott standards are turning into a checklist operators race to publicly sign onto, even though the underlying interconnection queue they're supposedly cleared for stays frozen until Aug. 20 at the earliest.</p>

      <hr class="section-divider" />

      <h2>🏗️ 2. Data Center Construction &amp; Corporate Activity</h2>
      <p class="section-tagline">Amazon Confirms Its 8,000-Acre West Texas Mega-Campus</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Amazon confirmed it has acquired the roughly 8,000-acre GW Ranch site in Pecos County from developer Pacifico Energy, following construction-permit filings earlier this month for three data-center buildings, with land-clearing already underway (Data Center Dynamics, Aug. 10; Yahoo Finance, Aug. 10).</li>
        <li>In a statement, Amazon said the campus is "powered by new on-site generation that won't raise electricity costs for Texas families" and is "designed to transition to grid-connected service as interconnection timelines allow" — pairing the announcement with the compliance language now expected under Abbott's standards.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="signal"><strong>Today's Signal:</strong> Amazon just closed the year's largest confirmed Texas data-center land deal in the same week the state paused new grid connections — a reminder that off-grid, self-generated campuses aren't waiting on ERCOT's timeline.</p>

      <hr class="section-divider" />

      <p>No new TCEQ draft permit, public-notice filing, or public-comment deadline tied to a data center or gas plant surfaced today in Ellis, Collin, Johnson, Denton, Wise, Tarrant, Parker, or Hood counties.</p>

      <hr class="section-divider" />

      <h2>🏛️ 3. Land-Use &amp; Zoning Developments</h2>
      <p class="section-tagline">Fort Worth Council Votes Tonight on a 90-Day Moratorium</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Fort Worth's City Council meets tonight, Aug. 11 — its first session since summer break — to vote on a 90-day moratorium on new data-center development applications (extendable for another 90 days), creation of a standing data-center review committee, and new mandatory filing requirements covering water-efficient cooling and usage reporting (Fort Worth Report, WFAA, City of Fort Worth council agenda).</li>
        <li>Because the Zoning Commission voted 7-4 in July to recommend denial of the city's original data-center ordinance, tonight's measures need a three-fourths council supermajority — at least 9 of 11 votes — to pass.</li>
        <li>The vote follows Denton City Council's Aug. 5 4-3 decision to direct staff toward its own data-center moratorium and zoning rewrite, with public hearings tentatively set for Sept. 22 and Oct. 27 and final adoption targeted for Dec. 2 (KERA News, WFAA, Community Impact, Aug. 5).</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="signal"><strong>Today's Signal:</strong> Two of DFW's largest cities are now moving toward outright pauses on new data-center development within the same two weeks, even as the state's own audit process is still working out what companies have to disclose.</p>

      <hr class="section-divider" />

      <h2>⚡ 4. Power-First Strategies &amp; On-Site Generation</h2>
      <p class="section-tagline">A 7.65-Gigawatt Gas Plant Anchors the Pecos County Campus</p>
      <ul>
        <li>The GW Ranch project centers on a 35-turbine, 7.65 GW natural-gas plant developed by Pacifico Energy under a TCEQ air permit — behind-the-meter capacity that, according to permit filings, could authorize up to 33 million tons of annual greenhouse gas emissions, potentially making it one of the largest natural-gas plants and single CO2 sources in the US (Tom's Hardware, Ars Technica, Data Center Dynamics, Aug. 10).</li>
        <li>Amazon says the site will combine the gas plant with solar and battery storage, following a similar behind-the-meter playbook Microsoft has already announced nearby with a 2.67 GW gas plant backing its own 2 GW Pecos-area development.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="signal"><strong>Today's Signal:</strong> Behind-the-meter gas is now the default answer for hyperscalers who don't want their multi-gigawatt Texas campuses waiting in ERCOT's paused queue.</p>

      <hr class="section-divider" />

      <h2>📍 5. Today's Watch List</h2>
      <p class="section-tagline">Actionable Signals for Tuesday, August 11</p>

      <h3>ERCOT</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Whether ERCOT or the PUCT posts any new Batch Zero classification notice ahead of the Aug. 20 open meeting</li>
        <li>Whether additional operators announce compliance with Abbott's Aug. 3 standards</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>PUC</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>PUCT's ruling on ERCOT's good-cause exception request at the Aug. 20 meeting</li>
        <li>Any public response from Abbott's office to Trump's criticism of the pause</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>TCEQ</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Any new draft permit or public-notice filing tied to a data center or gas plant in Ellis, Collin, Johnson, Denton, Wise, Tarrant, Parker, or Hood counties</li>
        <li>Whether TCEQ is formally looped into the PUCT/ERCOT audit process</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>County Zoning</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Outcome of Fort Worth's Aug. 11 vote on the moratorium, review committee, and filing-requirement package</li>
        <li>Next steps from Denton's Aug. 5 direction toward a moratorium and zoning rewrite</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Corporate Filings</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Any additional permitting detail tied to Amazon's Pecos County GW Ranch buildout</li>
        <li>Whether Microsoft, Meta, or other hyperscalers confirm comparable behind-the-meter gas deals elsewhere in Texas</li>
      </ul>

